  "residualRisks": [
    "This approval covers privacy engineering of the unchanged candidate authorities only. It is not a legal determination, catalog promotion, implementation admission, runtime permission or authority to process personal or tenant data.",
    "Prior privacy approval forge-contracts-6ec0959-privacy is reused explicitly by its immutable SHA-256 and identical source tree. Its storage suite, Missions/reconciliation suite and twenty-six independent probes were not rerun here. This pass independently recomputed hashes and retention/provenance assertions; it makes no fresh heavy-suite success claim.",
    "The prior PRIV-01 and PRIV-02 resolutions remain unchanged: a versioned ADR records the owner-selected design, and durable confirmation/release history remains separate from uncertain observations. No additional class, retention extension, owner, provider, audit role or interpretation is admitted by this attestation.",
    "Unknown outcomes protect the archive and package pending authenticated terminal reconciliation. Real consumers must prove discoverable reservations, finality fencing and reconciliation liveness; absent that evidence, unresolved retention cannot be treated as physically implemented or complete.",
    "The Specifications archive remains while-referenced then P5Y after terminal release, with P35D backups and no historical-pointer tail cascade. Seventeen inherited rules are unchanged. Calendar boundaries, physical purge, PostgreSQL RLS/races, migration and deletion-before-restore require actual consumer qualification.",
    "Historical acceptance evidence and preserved archive bytes do not restore current permission or allow planning after expiry. Current authority, revocation, explicit audit access and execution controls remain independent; fixtures and this review grant none of them.",
    "Clean tree equality establishes source equivalence between the reviewed commits, not remote PR admission or post-merge CI. The publication commit and any later Specification Lock retain their separate integration requirements."
  ],
